{"author":{"id":"1949ffc23f11a8cb4f8c42a45c9ad241b49570df9a18687489e9ea78d87c54a3"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1779639221,"nonce":"j8wNVA8JpzdHuCkDVk3ciXA/sUo=","title":"Audit: Feedbax/Studio issue partition, sequencing, and parallel work rules","message":"## Purpose\n\nCapture the 2026-05-24 Feedbax / Feedbax Studio planning audit: current issue partition, recent `develop` history, auth-closure cleanup findings, contract-first sequencing, and practical rules for parallel agent work.\n\nThis issue is a coordination artifact. It should guide work partitioning and handoffs; implementation should happen in the specific child/domain issues named below.\n\n## Inputs audited\n\n- The two design coordination issues: `8d94424` (Feedbax design coordination) and `93f79f2` (Feedbax Studio design coordination).\n- Open umbrella and coordination issues, including `a3e46d5`, `bd5aefb`, `6116155`, `d3f9af7`, `c77b227`.\n- First messages from Codex conversations in this repo since 2026-05-03.\n- Recent `develop` merge history through `87e973e`.\n- Closed issues attached to auth requests where closure may not have matched full issue scope.\n\n## Immediate ledger cleanup already done\n\nThree issues were found to have been closed by prior auth-request merges even though the branches only delivered partial slices or context work. They were reopened with corrective comments:\n\n- `0c46f69` — Studio pipeline workspace spec. Merge `b682122b` delivered selector/metrics work, but the issue's pre-closure status said the broader pipeline workspace spec remained open.\n- `85cec2c` — runtime/schema-backed state browser. Merge `b682122b` delivered the first curated selector picker, but not runtime/provider-backed PyTree schema browsing or validated selector lowering.\n- `0258fac` — port constraints/type checking. Merge `27384a2` delivered a substantial schema/dimension/recurrence slice, but residual child/parent work remains around visual affordances, compatibility explanations, registry metadata, and closure/rescope decisions.\n\nCross-project workflow evidence was recorded in Feedbax meta coordination issue `1c01192` and Mandible issue `7b83c1f` because the root workflow problem is that auth requests need separate reference-only issue associations and closing issue targets.\n\n## Key finding\n\nThe current critical path is contract-first. Recent work moved Feedbax Studio from a mostly frontend/product refactor into a shared contract stack:\n\n- `GraphSpec` as canonical model representation.\n- Provider-owned `ValueSchema`, `PortSchema`, `TaskDataSchema`, `SelectorTargetSchema` contracts.\n- Task Data v2 bindings and worker enforcement.\n- Explicit recurrent wire temporality and dimension propagation.\n- Retained observable / loss / artifact semantics.\n- Generic graph-template execution and worker lowering.\n- Studio workspace/stage/scenario specs and manifest/execution lineage.\n\nTherefore broad Studio UI redesign should not lead until the graph/schema/worker contracts it depends on are stable enough to consume.\n\n## Open issue partition\n\n### Coordination and umbrellas\n\n- `93f79f2` — Feedbax Studio design coordination.\n- `8d94424` — Feedbax design coordination.\n- `1c01192` — Feedbax project meta coordination for cross-project/workflow concerns.\n- `a3e46d5` — active Studio product-depth and Feedbax contract sequence.\n- `bd5aefb` — ValueSpec / timeline / expression-valued parameter system.\n- `6116155` — typed subgraph taxonomy and pluggable canvas architecture.\n- `d3f9af7` — autosave robustness.\n- `c77b227` — composite cleanup.\n\nClosed but still useful for orientation:\n\n- `fa3978f` — Feedbax/Mandible MVP roadmap.\n- `07c0ec9` — graph-template / staged-model deprecation umbrella.\n\n### Provider, manifests, execution, cross-repo contracts\n\n- `c6c6da0` — Feedbax manifests/provider contract depth.\n- `f68cf66` — Mandible provider/plugin boundary.\n- `e33f487` — canonical run manifests/indexing.\n- `63c798f` — provider depth for eval/analysis/report manifests, artifacts, actions.\n- `7944274` — live SSH/RunPod/Modal execution, logs, artifacts, manifests.\n- `5c6b294` — RLRMP seeded artifact loadability/path normalization.\n\n### Studio schema, selectors, recurrence, ValueSpec\n\n- `0258fac` — port constraints/type checking and compatibility UX.\n- `85cec2c` — runtime/schema-backed state browser.\n- `bd5aefb` — generalized value/function/distribution/schedule editor.\n- `974b9ba`, `0866f1d`, `75a4665` — schema vocabulary, dimension propagation, compatibility UX child track.\n- `7f02d30`, `f93db6a`, `56dfd97` — recurrent edge UX, backend recurrence contract, deferred inner-iteration semantics.\n- `d72b09a`, `cb8025f` — explicit retained observable UI and analysis observable inputs.\n\n### Studio workspace, pipeline, persistence, UI\n\n- `0c46f69` — Studio pipeline workspace.\n- `a3e46d5` — product-depth sequence.\n- `946c859` — Workspace spatial representation/objective authoring.\n- `6a094c9` — Workspace runtime playback/validation overlays.\n- `7044904` — stage badge placement, likely stale after `0d82869` but needs verification.\n- `d90b3e5` — training runs vs sessions/tabs.\n- `c7869b3`, `d3f9af7` — proactive graph persistence / autosave robustness.\n- `2ec5e0b`, `7d9dcae` — shelf layout and edge routing ergonomics.\n\n### Graph templates, components, registry, subgraphs\n\n- `4b7cc96`, `4f835cb` — generic graph worker execution and built-in graph templates; likely partly/completely implemented by `87e973e`, but verify residuals before closing/rescoping.\n- `a1036a6` — derive Channel input prototypes from source schema.\n- `0e1cd8c` — probes/metrics as retained observables / Data projection inventory.\n- `b3ad156` — flexible intervention targeting follow-through.\n- `6116155`, `2f8dd61`, `a6efafc`, `a8efe7e` — typed subgraph taxonomy, parametric composites, acausal/DAE subgraphs, context constraints.\n- `c77b227`, `afff745`, `44183de`, `d0cc2b7`, palette/registry cleanup issues — cleanup after contract blockers.\n- `e8ec78e`, `f22dbf4` — long-term analysis/model graph composition.\n\n### Training, RL, execution correctness\n\n- `67e2e5e`, `3014308`, `c9ae44f`, `32d0935`, `a39e7d0`, `ba00cef`, `f90e6e2`.\n- `ae2d692` — checkpoint collisions.\n- `96a0725`, `29c89d8` — static JAX metadata recompilation issue and diagnosis.\n- `cc62b31`, `4628cbc`, `fe093fc`, `6fe14ab` — cloud/runtime reliability, CDE stability, MJX, morphology.\n\n### Analysis/statistics/scientific layer\n\n- `b2db05b`, `c722539`, `4cb86c8` — Statistics tab backend/frontend/product split.\n- `e8452a4`, `3fae4f8`, `3fc3e8e`, `7c0c4b2`, `96fa62f`, `2aa4ae4`, `e0686e7`, `c1ad986` — analysis migration, analytical lowering, variable trials, selectors, Treescope, named axes, NN/Penzai uniformity.\n\n### Legacy core/API backlog and ops/tooling\n\nLegacy core/API backlog should be opportunistic unless it blocks the contract lane: `208a5f0`, `84c3ec4`, `07d8897`, `f451fc4`, `ad4401d`, `3c5dbe3`, `c39271a`, `e7b6e26`, `d999dea`, `6d59f0c`, `323de09`, `56c23c8`, `e919d5d`, `b59c952`, `f385489`, `9480bb3`, `5875eb6`, `fb6fffc`, `e438b89`, `138c2d3`, `5f22fa7`, `bdee8d1`.\n\nOps/tooling issues: `62920be`, `7f7b9a9`, `b875445`, `5d16d61`, `8cc42a3`, `c172b2a`.\n\n## Recommended sequence\n\n1. Finish ledger hygiene first. Verify stale-open candidates: `7044904`, `974b9ba`, `0866f1d`, `75a4665`, `4b7cc96`, `4f835cb`, `d72b09a`, `7f02d30`, `f93db6a`. Read-only verification can be parallelized, but issue writes should be serialized.\n2. Run one serialized contract integration lane for graph/runtime/worker/retention: `0e1cd8c`, `4b7cc96`, `a1036a6`, `f93db6a`, `7f02d30`, `cb8025f`.\n3. Run provider/schema/task-data compatibility next or in the same integration branch: `c6c6da0`, reopened `0258fac`, `85cec2c`, and children `974b9ba`, `0866f1d`, `75a4665`.\n4. After selector/retention semantics settle, take `bd5aefb`: generalized `StudioValueSpec` / expression / function / distribution / schedule editing.\n5. After step 2, provider/execution depth can proceed with care: `e33f487` and `63c798f` are one Feedbax manifest/provider lane; `7944274` can start once execution specs are stable; `f68cf66` can run in Mandible as a consumer/integration lane once Feedbax provider shape is clear. `c6c6da0` overlaps schema contracts and should serialize with step 3.\n6. After steps 2-4, run Studio product breadth: `0c46f69`, `a3e46d5`, `946c859`, `6a094c9`, `d90b3e5`, autosave/persistence, and canvas ergonomics.\n7. Safe parallel lanes now: analysis/statistics, isolated RL/training correctness, and mechanics/plant work, provided they avoid shared Studio/provider contracts.\n8. Treat legacy/core backlog and ops issues as opportunistic cleanup unless they block current work.\n\n## Parallel work rule\n\nOnly one lane should mutate shared Feedbax Studio contracts at a time: `GraphSpec`, provider/schema models, task-data bindings, worker lowering, retained observables, saved workspace/project schemas, and the matching TypeScript types/stores. Work touching `feedbax/studio_schema.py`, `feedbax/provider.py`, `feedbax/web/models/graph.py`, `feedbax/web/serialization.py`, `feedbax/web/worker/*`, `feedbax/graph_templates.py`, `feedbax/retained_observables.py`, `web/src/types/*`, `web/src/features/schema/*`, `web/src/features/scenario/taskBindings.ts`, `web/src/stores/workspaceStore.ts`, or `web/src/stores/graphStore.ts` belongs to the contract lane and should be merged through one integration branch.\n\nParallel agents should either work read-only, or own isolated lanes: pure UI ergonomics that do not change saved spec meaning, backend analysis/mechanics/RL work that avoids Studio/provider contracts, or Mandible/provider-ingest work that consumes already-stable Feedbax outputs. If an agent discovers it must change the shared contract surface, it should stop and report rather than quietly expanding its lane.\n\n## Notes on partitioning\n\nThe ledger-hygiene list in step 1 is not one implementation chunk. It should be verified, then repartitioned by overlap: schema/dimension issues together, graph-template/observable issues together, and simple stale UI closure separately. Do not hand each issue to a separate implementation agent until the residual scope is known, because several are parent/child or duplicate bookkeeping around the same merged work.","files":null}]}